00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.430
Kicking off at number 5 is Mowgli's meeting
with King Louie.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.629
The man-cub is kidnapped by a pack of monkeys
who take him to their leader, who's played

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:47.680
like a mob boss by actor Christopher Walken.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.449
I love the fact that, at first, we only see
a little bit of King Louie as he talks to

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:52.770
Mowgli from the shadows.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:58.149
It really makes the reveal of what an absolute
beast of an ape he is more effective.

00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:02.609
This Gigantopithecus is desperate for Mowgli
to give him the secret of fire, or man's red

00:01:02.609 --> 00:01:03.840
flower, as he calls it.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.409
Cue a few verses of "I Wanna Be Like You",
before Baloo interrupts to try and rescue

00:01:08.409 --> 00:01:09.409
Mowgli.

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:13.590
Baloo's over-the-top talk about how big a
fan he is of King Louie is really funny.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.250
Then Mowgli makes a run for it, and Baloo
and Bagheera battle a bunch of monkeys, while

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:21.940
the King-Kong-like Louie chases the man-cub
through the stone temple, which soon comes

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.860
crashing down around him.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.210
Pretty dramatic stuff!

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.920
At number 4 is the movie's big action-packed
finale!

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.049
When Bagheera confirms that Shere Khan has
killed Mowgli's adoptive wolf-father, Akela,

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:37.490
the man-cub runs off to the village where
he grabs a flaming torch before racing back

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.090
into the jungle, which he accidentally sets
on fire.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:44.729
But the jungle fire is only one of Mowgli's
problems; Shere Khan's also ready to take

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:49.920
him down once and for all, but Mowgli's animal
friends step up and confront the terrifying

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.700
tiger in a series of clashes.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.440
I like the fact that meanwhile Mowgli's following
Bagheera's advice and using his human skills

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.330
to set a trap to bring down Shere Khan.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:03.950
And after the tiger chases Mowgli into the
blazing jungle and up a dead tree, it's not

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:08.320
long before Shere Khan exits this world the
same way many a Disney villain has bitten

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.680
the dust over the years: Hans Gruber-style!

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.130
And, I've gotta say, that I much prefer the
way this movie ends, with Mowgli staying in

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:18.480
the jungle with his friends and family, rather
than chasing after a girl as he does in the

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.400
1967 animated film.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.440
I really love the movie's closing credits,
which make it in to my list at number 3.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.410
They start off with The Jungle Book novel
jumping about, then it's flung open to reveal

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.330
the ruins of King Louie's temple.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.570
The gigantic orangutan bursts out singing
"I Wanna Be Like You", which continues as

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:40.290
the pages of the book turn and we see the
animals including Bagheera, Baloo, and Shere

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.450
Khan pop up out of the pages in all their
photo-realistic glory.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:48.650
Then they appear as beautiful hand-drawn-style
pictures alongside their voice actors' names,

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.730
and then it's back to photo-real creatures
including turtles crawling across the pages

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:53.730
of The Jungle Book.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.740
And we also get to hear Scarlett Johansson's
hypnotic version of 'Trust In Me', Kaa's song

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.380
from the 1967 movie, which I really wish they'd
included in her part of the movie.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.990
And there's also a cool version of Bare Necessities
by Dr John and the Nite Trippers.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:11.270
The credits really are an absolute imaginative
delight.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.650
At number 2 is Mowgli's first face-off with
Shere Khan.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:18.880
Following the tiger's threats, Bagheera accompanies
the man-cub on his journey to leave the jungle.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.990
Despite the initial quiet of the grassland
they're crossing, Bagheera warns Mowgli to

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.740
run to the ravine as he senses something's
not right.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.630
Cue Shere Khan leaping out of the gently swaying
grass and tackling Bagheera before chasing

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:35.560
Mowgli through the field which is now full
of stampeding buffalo who race into the muddy

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.920
ravine where Mowgli is, hurtling towards our
favourite man-cub!

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:42.960
Shere Khan watches from above, at the edge
of the ravine, as Mowgli escapes by grabbing

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.620
on to the horns of one of the buffalo rushing
by him.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.110
The scene's really fast-paced and immersive,
so much so that you can almost feel the mud

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:53.021
as it flies everywhere, including on to the
camera lens!

00:03:53.021 --> 00:03:56.900
And at number 1 is Baloo's endless quest for
honey!

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.670
After the lazy-bones bear saves Mowgli from
Kaa, he sets the young human to work to pay

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:06.370
off his debt for being rescued by shimmying
up a huge rock face to collect honey from

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:07.840
the bee-hives at the top.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.890
It's funny hearing Baloo assure Mowgli that
these particular bees don't sting.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:16.320
And it's even funnier when our loveable liar's
joined by a bunch of other animals who comment

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.290
on how Mowgli's doing and we find out that
Baloo's tried this many times before with

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.750
many monkeys who all skedaddled as soon as
they were stung.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.590
Oh, and when a huge piece of honey falls on
Fred the pygmy hog, it's so funny when Baloo

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.210
picks him up and licks the honey off his back!

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:36.560
Later, Mowgli makes himself a protective suit
of leaves and tools to do the job more efficiently,

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.510
while Baloo lies back, scratching his belly
until it's time to put into action Mowgli's

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.430
ingenious new honey-gathering technique.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:47.760
Then Baloo goes back to a lazy day floating
down the river, singing Bare Necessities,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.420
while Mowgli sits on his tum.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.000
Ok, what are your favourite moments from The
